#: src/features/RolePlay/scenarios/small-talk-with-a-stranger.ts:13
#. js-lingui-explicit-id
msgid ""
"Talking to a stranger can feel uncomfortable because neither person knows "
"what to say yet. The goal is not to sound especially clever or interesting. "
"Your first goal is simply to make the other person feel comfortable and "
"discover something you can talk about together.\n"
"\n"
"The advice below is inspired by ideas from Charles Duhigg’s book "
"*Supercommunicators*.\n"
"\n"
"## How to Start a Conversation\n"
"\n"
"Use the situation you already share. You do not need an original or "
"impressive opening line.\n"
"\n"
"Try making a simple observation and adding an easy question:\n"
"\n"
"- “This place is busier than I expected. Have you been here before?”\n"
"- “How do you know the host?”\n"
"- “What brought you to this event?”\n"
"- “That looks interesting. What are you drinking?”\n"
"- “Is this your first time at an event like this?”\n"
"\n"
"Questions about the immediate situation feel natural because they give the "
"other person an obvious way to respond.\n"
"\n"
"## Ask Questions That Reveal More Than Facts\n"
"\n"
"Basic questions are useful for starting a conversation:\n"
"\n"
"- “Where are you from?”\n"
"- “What do you do?”\n"
"- “Do you live nearby?”\n"
"\n"
"However, a conversation becomes more interesting when you gently ask about "
"the person’s experiences, feelings, or preferences.\n"
"\n"
"Instead of only asking:\n"
"\n"
"> “What do you do for work?”\n"
"\n"
"You could continue with:\n"
"\n"
"> “What do you enjoy most about it?”\n"
"\n"
"Instead of:\n"
"\n"
"> “How long have you lived here?”\n"
"\n"
"Try:\n"
"\n"
"> “What do you like most about living here?”\n"
"\n"
"Other useful questions include:\n"
"\n"
"- “How did you become interested in that?”\n"
"- “What has been the best part of your week?”\n"
"- “What do you usually enjoy doing on weekends?”\n"
"- “What is something you are looking forward to?”\n"
"- “What kind of places do you like visiting?”\n"
"\n"
"These questions are personal enough to create a connection, but still safe "
"and appropriate for someone you have just met.\n"
"\n"
"## Listen for the Kind of Conversation They Want\n"
"\n"
"People do not always want the same thing from a conversation. They may want "
"to:\n"
"\n"
"1. Exchange information or make a decision.\n"
"2. Share an emotion or experience.\n"
"3. Talk about their interests, identity, or relationships.\n"
"\n"
"Try to respond to the kind of conversation the other person has started.\n"
"\n"
"If someone says:\n"
"\n"
"> “I moved here two weeks ago and everything still feels unfamiliar.”\n"
"\n"
"A purely practical response might be:\n"
"\n"
"> “You should download a city guide.”\n"
"\n"
"But an emotional response may connect better:\n"
"\n"
"> “That sounds exciting, but probably a little overwhelming too. How has it "
"been so far?”\n"
"\n"
"You do not always need to solve a problem. Sometimes people simply want to "
"feel understood.\n"
"\n"
"## Show That You Are Really Listening\n"
"\n"
"A useful technique is to:\n"
"\n"
"1. Ask a question.\n"
"2. Listen carefully.\n"
"3. Summarize the important part in your own words.\n"
"4. Check whether you understood correctly.\n"
"\n"
"For example:\n"
"\n"
"> “So you started learning photography because you wanted a creative hobby "
"outside work. Did I understand that correctly?”\n"
"\n"
"You do not need to do this formally after every answer. Short natural "
"responses also work:\n"
"\n"
"- “So that is what brought you here.”\n"
"- “It sounds like you really enjoyed it.”\n"
"- “I can see why that was frustrating.”\n"
"- “So you prefer quieter places?”\n"
"- “That makes sense.”\n"
"\n"
"These responses show that you are not simply waiting for your turn to "
"speak.\n"
"\n"
"## Share Something About Yourself Too\n"
"\n"
"Good small talk should not feel like an interview. After asking a question, "
"share a related detail about yourself.\n"
"\n"
"For example:\n"
"\n"
"> “What kind of music do you listen to? I’ve been listening to a lot of "
"jazz recently, but I’m trying to discover something new.”\n"
"\n"
"Or:\n"
"\n"
"> “Have you travelled anywhere interesting recently? I visited a small town "
"by a lake last weekend and really enjoyed it.”\n"
"\n"
"A useful pattern is:\n"
"\n"
"**Ask → Listen → Respond → Share → Ask again**\n"
"\n"
"This creates a balanced conversation in which both people gradually reveal "
"more about themselves.\n"
"\n"
"## How to Keep the Conversation Going\n"
"\n"
"Listen for a detail you can explore.\n"
"\n"
"If the person says:\n"
"\n"
"> “I recently started running.”\n"
"\n"
"You could ask:\n"
"\n"
"- “What made you start?”\n"
"- “Do you prefer running alone or with other people?”\n"
"- “Was it difficult in the beginning?”\n"
"- “Are you training for anything?”\n"
"\n"
"You do not need to introduce a completely new topic after every answer. "
"Often, the easiest way to continue is to become curious about one detail "
"the person has already mentioned.\n"
"\n"
"## What to Avoid\n"
"\n"
"Try not to:\n"
"\n"
"- Ask many unrelated questions one after another.\n"
"- Turn every answer back to yourself.\n"
"- Give advice before understanding what the person needs.\n"
"- Ask questions that are too personal too quickly.\n"
"- Pretend to understand when you are confused.\n"
"- Focus so much on perfect grammar that you stop listening.\n"
"\n"
"It is completely natural to pause, search for a word, or ask someone to "
"repeat themselves.\n"
"\n"
"Useful phrases include:\n"
"\n"
"- “Sorry, could you say that again?”\n"
"- “What do you mean by that?”\n"
"- “I’m not sure I understood correctly.”\n"
"- “How would you describe it?”\n"
"- “Give me a second—I’m trying to find the right word.”\n"
"\n"
"## How to End the Conversation Politely\n"
"\n"
"Not every conversation needs to last a long time. You can finish warmly "
"without making the moment awkward:\n"
"\n"
"- “It was really nice talking to you.”\n"
"- “I’m going to get another drink, but I’m glad we met.”\n"
"- “I’ll let you talk to the others, but I enjoyed our conversation.”\n"
"- “I hope you enjoy the rest of the event.”\n"
"- “Maybe I’ll see you again later.”\n"
"\n"
"Ending a conversation politely is also an important communication skill.\n"
"\n"
"## A Simple Conversation Formula\n"
"\n"
"When you are unsure what to do, remember:\n"
"\n"
"1. **Open:** Comment on the shared situation.\n"
"2. **Explore:** Ask about an experience, preference, or feeling.\n"
"3. **Listen:** Respond to something specific they said.\n"
"4. **Connect:** Share a related detail about yourself.\n"
"5. **Continue or close:** Ask a follow-up question or end politely.\n"
"\n"
"## Practice Scenario\n"
"\n"
"In this roleplay, you are meeting a friendly stranger at a social event. "
"Start with an easy question, listen for interesting details, and gradually "
"move from basic facts to experiences and interests.\n"
"\n"
"Try to practice at least three skills:\n"
"\n"
"- Ask one question about an experience or feeling.\n"
"- Refer to something the stranger said earlier.\n"
"- Share something related about yourself.\n"
"\n"
"Do not worry about creating a perfect conversation. Concentrate on being "
"curious, attentive, and easy to talk to."
msgstr ""
"Hablar con un desconocido puede sentirse incómodo porque ninguna de las "
"personas sabe qué decir aún. El objetivo no es sonar especialmente "
"inteligente o interesante. Tu primer objetivo es simplemente hacer que la "
"otra persona se sienta cómoda y descubrir algo de lo que puedan hablar "
"juntos.\n"
"\n"
"El consejo a continuación está inspirado por ideas del libro de Charles "
"Duhigg *Supercommunicators*.\n"
"\n"
"## Cómo Iniciar una Conversación\n"
"\n"
"Usa la situación que ya comparten. No necesitas una línea de apertura "
"original o impresionante.\n"
"\n"
"Intenta hacer una observación simple y añadir una pregunta fácil:\n"
"\n"
"- \"Este lugar está más concurrido de lo que esperaba. ¿Has estado aquí "
"antes?\"\n"
"- \"¿Cómo conoces al anfitrión?\"\n"
"- \"¿Qué te trajo a este evento?\"\n"
"- \"Eso parece interesante. ¿Qué estás bebiendo?\"\n"
"- \"¿Es tu primera vez en un evento como este?\"\n"
"\n"
"Las preguntas sobre la situación inmediata se sienten naturales porque le "
"dan a la otra persona una forma obvia de responder.\n"
"\n"
"## Haz Preguntas Que Revelen Más Que Hechos\n"
"\n"
"Las preguntas básicas son útiles para iniciar una conversación:\n"
"\n"
"- \"¿De dónde eres?\"\n"
"- \"¿A qué te dedicas?\"\n"
"- \"¿Vives cerca?\"\n"
"\n"
"Sin embargo, una conversación se vuelve más interesante cuando preguntas "
"suavemente sobre las experiencias, sentimientos o preferencias de la "
"persona.\n"
"\n"
"En lugar de solo preguntar:\n"
"\n"
"> \"¿A qué te dedicas?\"\n"
"\n"
"Podrías continuar con:\n"
"\n"
"> \"¿Qué es lo que más disfrutas de eso?\"\n"
"\n"
"En lugar de:\n"
"\n"
"> \"¿Cuánto tiempo llevas viviendo aquí?\"\n"
"\n"
"Prueba con:\n"
"\n"
"> \"¿Qué es lo que más te gusta de vivir aquí?\"\n"
"\n"
"Otras preguntas útiles incluyen:\n"
"\n"
"- \"¿Cómo te interesaste en eso?\"\n"
"- \"¿Cuál ha sido la mejor parte de tu semana?\"\n"
"- \"¿Qué sueles disfrutar hacer los fines de semana?\"\n"
"- \"¿Qué es algo que esperas con ansias?\"\n"
"- \"¿Qué tipo de lugares te gusta visitar?\"\n"
"\n"
"Estas preguntas son lo suficientemente personales como para crear una "
"conexión, pero siguen siendo seguras y apropiadas para alguien que acabas "
"de conocer.\n"
"\n"
"## Escucha el Tipo de Conversación Que Quieren Ellos\n"
"\n"
"Las personas no siempre quieren lo mismo de una conversación. Pueden "
"querer:\n"
"\n"
"1. Intercambiar información o tomar una decisión.\n"
"2. Compartir una emoción o experiencia.\n"
"3. Hablar sobre sus intereses, identidad o relaciones.\n"
"\n"
"Intenta responder al tipo de conversación que la otra persona ha comenzado.\n"
"\n"
"Si alguien dice:\n"
"\n"
"> \"Me mudé aquí hace dos semanas y todo todavía se siente desconocido.\"\n"
"\n"
"Una respuesta puramente práctica podría ser:\n"
"\n"
"> \"Deberías descargar una guía de la ciudad.\"\n"
"\n"
"Pero una respuesta emocional puede conectar mejor:\n"
"\n"
"> \"Eso suena emocionante, pero probablemente un poco abrumador también. "
"¿Cómo ha sido hasta ahora?\"\n"
"\n"
"No siempre necesitas resolver un problema. A veces las personas simplemente "
"quieren sentirse comprendidas.\n"
"\n"
"## Demuestra Que Realmente Estás Escuchando\n"
"\n"
"Una técnica útil es:\n"
"\n"
"1. Hacer una pregunta.\n"
"2. Escuchar cuidadosamente.\n"
"3. Resumir la parte importante en tus propias palabras.\n"
"4. Comprobar si entendiste correctamente.\n"
"\n"
"Por ejemplo:\n"
"\n"
"> \"Entonces empezaste a aprender fotografía porque querías un pasatiempo "
"creativo fuera del trabajo. ¿Entendí bien?\"\n"
"\n"
"No necesitas hacer esto formalmente después de cada respuesta. Las "
"respuestas cortas y naturales también funcionan:\n"
"\n"
"- \"Así que eso fue lo que te trajo aquí.\"\n"
"- \"Parece que realmente lo disfrutaste.\"\n"
"- \"Puedo ver por qué eso fue frustrante.\"\n"
"- \"¿Entonces prefieres lugares más tranquilos?\"\n"
"- \"Tiene sentido.\"\n"
"\n"
"Estas respuestas muestran que no estás simplemente esperando tu turno para "
"hablar.\n"
"\n"
"## Comparte Algo de Ti También\n"
"\n"
"Una buena charla informal no debería sentirse como una entrevista. Después "
"de hacer una pregunta, comparte un detalle relacionado sobre ti.\n"
"\n"
"Por ejemplo:\n"
"\n"
"> \"¿Qué tipo de música escuchas? He estado escuchando mucho jazz "
"últimamente, pero estoy tratando de descubrir algo nuevo.\"\n"
"\n"
"O:\n"
"\n"
"> \"¿Has viajado a algún lugar interesante recientemente? Visité un pequeño "
"pueblo junto a un lago el fin de semana pasado y realmente lo disfruté.\"\n"
"\n"
"Un patrón útil es:\n"
"\n"
"**Pregunta → Escucha → Responde → Comparte → Pregunta de nuevo**\n"
"\n"
"Esto crea una conversación equilibrada en la que ambas personas van "
"revelando gradualmente más sobre sí mismas.\n"
"\n"
"## Cómo Mantener la Conversación en Marcha\n"
"\n"
"Escucha un detalle que puedas explorar.\n"
"\n"
"Si la persona dice:\n"
"\n"
"> \"Recientemente comencé a correr.\"\n"
"\n"
"Podrías preguntar:\n"
"\n"
"- \"¿Qué te hizo comenzar?\"\n"
"- \"¿Prefieres correr solo o con otras personas?\"\n"
"- \"¿Fue difícil al principio?\"\n"
"- \"¿Estás entrenando para algo?\"\n"
"\n"
"No necesitas introducir un tema completamente nuevo después de cada "
"respuesta. A menudo, la manera más fácil de continuar es volverse curioso "
"sobre un detalle que la persona ya ha mencionado.\n"
"\n"
"## Qué Evitar\n"
"\n"
"Intenta no:\n"
"\n"
"- Hacer muchas preguntas no relacionadas una tras otra.\n"
"- Volver cada respuesta hacia ti mismo.\n"
"- Dar consejos antes de entender lo que la persona necesita.\n"
"- Hacer preguntas que sean demasiado personales demasiado rápido.\n"
"- Fingir entender cuando estás confundido.\n"
"- Enfocarte tanto en la gramática perfecta que dejes de escuchar.\n"
"\n"
"Es completamente natural pausar, buscar una palabra o pedirle a alguien que "
"repita.\n"
"\n"
"Frases útiles incluyen:\n"
"\n"
"- \"Lo siento, ¿podrías decir eso de nuevo?\"\n"
"- \"¿Qué quieres decir con eso?\"\n"
"- \"No estoy seguro si entendí correctamente.\"\n"
"- \"¿Cómo lo describirías?\"\n"
"- \"Dame un segundo, estoy tratando de encontrar la palabra adecuada.\"\n"
"\n"
"## Cómo Terminar la Conversación Amablemente\n"
"\n"
"No todas las conversaciones necesitan durar mucho tiempo. Puedes terminar "
"calurosamente sin hacer el momento incómodo:\n"
"\n"
"- \"Realmente fue agradable hablar contigo.\"\n"
"- \"Voy a buscar otra bebida, pero me alegra que nos hayamos conocido.\"\n"
"- \"Te dejaré hablar con los demás, pero disfruté nuestra conversación.\"\n"
"- \"Espero que disfrutes el resto del evento.\"\n"
"- \"Quizás te vea de nuevo más tarde.\"\n"
"\n"
"Terminar una conversación amablemente también es una habilidad de "
"comunicación importante.\n"
"\n"
"## Una Fórmula Sencilla de Conversación\n"
"\n"
"Cuando no estés seguro de qué hacer, recuerda:\n"
"\n"
"1. **Abrir:** Comentar sobre la situación compartida.\n"
"2. **Explorar:** Preguntar sobre una experiencia, preferencia o "
"sentimiento.\n"
"3. **Escuchar:** Responder a algo específico que dijeron.\n"
"4. **Conectar:** Compartir un detalle relacionado sobre ti.\n"
"5. **Continuar o cerrar:** Hacer una pregunta de seguimiento o acabar "
"amablemente.\n"
"\n"
"## Escenario de Práctica\n"
"\n"
"En este roleplay, estás conociendo a un extraño amigable en un evento "
"social. Comienza con una pregunta fácil, escucha detalles interesantes y "
"muévete gradualmente de hechos básicos a experiencias e intereses.\n"
"\n"
"Intenta practicar al menos tres habilidades:\n"
"\n"
"- Haz una pregunta sobre una experiencia o sentimiento.\n"
"- Refiérete a algo que el extraño dijo antes.\n"
"- Comparte algo relacionado sobre ti.\n"
"\n"
"No te preocupes por crear una conversación perfecta. Concéntrate en ser "
"curioso, atento y fácil de hablar."

